[{"page":1,"text":"MINUTES OF A SPECIAL SESSION\n\nCITY COUNCIL MEETING\n\nJanuary 20, 2026, at 5:00 PM\n\nPresent\n\nAbsent\n\nMayor Zack Stratton\n\nCouncilmember Parker McCumber\n\nCouncilmember Jacob Wood\n\nCouncilmember Jacob Holdaway\n\nCouncilmember David Lauret\n\nCouncilmember Ezra Nair (Arrived at 5:17pm)\nStaff Present: City Manager Eric Ellis, Chief Deputy Holden Rockwell with the Utah County\nSheriff’s Office, Communications Director Jenna Ahern, Parks and Rec Director Brian Vawdrey,\n\nPublic Works Director Naseem Ghandour, and Deputy City Recorder Tony Lara\n\nOthers Speaking: Vineyard Residents\n\n1.\n\nCALL TO ORDER/INVOCATION/INSPIRATIONAL THOUGHT/PLEDGE OF\n\nALLEGIANCE\nMayor Stratton called the meeting to order at 5:00pm. Vineyard resident Mack Fellberg\ngave the opening prayer and led the pledge of allegiance.\n\n2.\n\nWORK SESSION\n\n2.1. City Council Listening Session for Vineyard Interfaith and Non-Profit\n\nOrganizations\n\nCouncilmember Holdaway will facilitate a listening session for the purpose of\ngathering input and exploring collaboration in the following areas:\n• The City’s Emergency Preparedness Plan\n• Strengthening a compassionate community response, including ways faithbased groups can provide practical support to first responders during\nemergencies\n• Ideas for expanding and improving service projects within the City\n• Opportunities to better involve faith-based and non-profit organizations in\n\nCity events, such as the Christmas Tree Lighting, Easter Egg Hunt, 9/11 Day of\n\nService, and Vineyard Heritage Days\n\nPage 1 of 3; January 20, 2026, City Council Minutes","ocr":false},{"page":2,"text":"Councilmember Holdaway presented on his item. He expressed his desire to have the city\nreach out to faith based and non-profit organizations to meet and discuss ways they and the city can\npartner together better. The council discussed the item, with councilmembers expressing support for\nthe idea and Mayor Stratton directing Councilmember Holdaway to coordinate with staff to set a\ntime for a meeting with the proposed groups.\n\n3.\n\nPUBLIC COMMENTS\n\nKim Cornelius, living in the Villas Subdivision, expressed concerns with recent public comments\nthat had been directed at specific councilmembers. He wished to remind the public to address issues\nand not specific individuals.\nMark Seastran with the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints and Nancy Hale with\nJustServe Utah expressed support for Councilmember Holdaway’s proposed listening session and\nexplained more on what each of their organizations do in coordination with the city.\nDaria Evans, living in the Villas Subdivision, expressed her gratitude for the Mayor’s inclusion of\nresidents and youth in offering a prayer and leading the pledge before the beginning of council\nmeetings.\n\n4.\n\nAPPOINTMENTS/REMOVALS\n\nThere were no appointments made during this meeting.\n\n5.\n\nBUSINESS ITEMS\n\n5.1. 300 West Parking Updates Resolution 2026-03\n\nDISCUSSION AND ACTION: The Council will discuss existing no-parking and\novernight parking restrictions along the full length of 300 West, including Vineyard\n\nLoop Road and South 300 West, and consideration of modifying or removing current\nsignage to allow general on-street parking where capacity exists.\n\nCouncilmember Wood presented the proposed changes.\nThe council discussed the proposed changes and expressed their support for the resolution.\n\nChief Deputy Holden Rockwell explained the current model regarding parking enforcement\nand asked for a clarification on what the changes would mean for his department from an\nenforcement perspective. A discussion ensued involving both Council and city staff. The result\nof the discussion was that the resolution would be amended to include protection for emergency\nparking as well as continued pool access for residents.\nMotion: COUNCILMEMBER HOLDAWAY MOVED TO ADOPT RESOLUTION 202603 WITH THE PROPOSED AMENDMENTS. COUNCILMEMBER NAIR SECONDED. THE\n\nROLL CALL WAS AS FOLLOWS: COUNCILMEMBERS MCCUMBER, HOLDAWAY,\nPage 2 of 3; January 20, 2026, City Council Minutes","ocr":false},{"page":3,"text":"WOOD, LAURET AND NAIR VOTED IN FAVOR. THE MOTION PASSED\n\nUNANIMOUSLY.\n\n5.2. Overnight Parking Permit Area at 100 West and 470 North (The Springs).\n\nResolution 2026-02\n\nDISCUSSION AND ACTION: Discussion of the existing overnight parking permit\nand restricted parking designation at 100 West and 470 North in The Springs\nneighborhood, including consideration of modifying or removing the current permit\narea and associated signage. The council may act to adopt (or deny) the resolution.\n\nCouncilmember Wood presented the proposed resolution.\nThe council along with staff discussed the proposed changes. This included a discussion\naround seeking input from the neighborhood the proposed changes would affect before a vote to\nadopt any changes. Councilmember Wood offered to circulate a survey of those residents to\nbetter inform the council.\n\nMotion: COUNCILMEMBER NAIR MOVED TO CONTINUE THE ITEM UNTIL\n\nAFTER THE COUNCIL WAS ABLE TO SURVEY THE IMPACTED NEIGHBORHOOD.\n\nCOUNCILMEMBER LAURET SECONDED. COUNCILMEMBERS NAIR, LAURET,\n\nWOOD, HOLDAWAY, MCCUMBER VOTED IN FAVOR. THE MOTION PASSED\n\nUNANIMOUSLY.\n\n6.\n\nCLOSED SESSION\n\nThere was no closed session held during this meeting.\n\n7.\n\nADJOURNMENT\n\nThe meeting was adjourned at 6:07pm\n\nMINUTES APPROVED ON:\n\n1/27/2026\n\nCERTIFIED CORRECT BY:\n\nTONY LARA, DEPUTY CITY RECORDER\n\nPage 3 of 3; January 20, 2026, City Council Minutes","ocr":false}]